# Error Handling Patterns ✅

## Core Error Handling Philosophy

### Graceful Degradation Over Crashes
The Juno app prioritizes **graceful degradation** over application crashes. Users should always have a functional experience, even when certain features are unavailable.

## Error Types and Handling

### 1. Permission-Related Errors
See [macOS Permission Handling](mdc:.cursor/rules/macos-permission-handling.mdc) for detailed patterns.

**Pattern**: Optional functionality with helpful guidance
```rust
match state.try_get_desktop() {
    Some(desktop) => {
        // Perform operation
        Ok(result)
    }
    None => {
        warn!("Operation requires accessibility permissions");
        Err("Desktop automation not available. Please grant accessibility permissions in System Preferences > Security & Privacy > Accessibility.".to_string())
    }
}
```

### 2. Agent System Errors - [AgentError](mdc:src-tauri/src/agent/mod.rs)
```rust
pub enum AgentError {
    ApiError(String),
    ToolError(String),
    TimeoutError(String),
    MemoryError(String),
    ConfigurationError(String),
}
```

**Implementation Pattern**:
```rust
use tracing::{error, warn, info, debug};

match agent_operation().await {
    Ok(result) => {
        info!("Agent operation completed successfully");
        Ok(result)
    }
    Err(AgentError::ApiError(e)) => {
        error!("API error in agent operation: {}", e);
        Err(format!("AI service temporarily unavailable: {}", e))
    }
    Err(AgentError::ToolError(e)) => {
        warn!("Tool execution failed: {}", e);
        Err(format!("Operation failed: {}", e))
    }
    Err(e) => {
        error!("Unexpected error: {:?}", e);
        Err("An unexpected error occurred. Please try again.".to_string())
    }
}
```

### 3. Command Result Types
**Standard Pattern**: All Tauri commands return `Result<T, String>`
```rust
#[tauri::command]
pub async fn command_name(state: State<'_, AppState>) -> Result<ResponseType, String> {
    // Implementation with proper error handling
}
```

### 4. Async Error Handling
```rust
use tokio::time::{timeout, Duration};

async fn robust_async_operation() -> Result<T, String> {
    match timeout(Duration::from_secs(30), operation()).await {
        Ok(Ok(result)) => Ok(result),
        Ok(Err(e)) => {
            error!("Operation failed: {}", e);
            Err(format!("Operation failed: {}", e))
        }
        Err(_) => {
            warn!("Operation timed out");
            Err("Operation timed out. Please try again.".to_string())
        }
    }
}
```

## Logging Strategy

### Log Levels and Usage
```rust
use tracing::{error, warn, info, debug, trace};

// ERROR: System failures, crashes, permission denied
error!("Critical system failure: {}", error_message);

// WARN: Degraded functionality, missing permissions, recoverable issues
warn!("Desktop automation unavailable, continuing with limited functionality");

// INFO: Normal operations, state changes, user actions
info!("Agent operation completed successfully");

// DEBUG: Development information, API payloads, detailed flow
debug!("API request payload: {:?}", request);

// TRACE: Very detailed execution flow (rarely used)
trace!("Entering function with parameters: {:?}", params);
```

### Context in Error Messages
**Good**: Include context and guidance
```rust
Err(format!("Failed to capture screenshot: {}. Please ensure accessibility permissions are granted in System Preferences > Security & Privacy > Accessibility.", e))
```

**Bad**: Generic error without context
```rust
Err("Screenshot failed".to_string())
```

## Frontend Error Handling

### React Error Boundaries
```typescript
// In UI components
const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

try {
    const result = await invoke('command_name');
    setError(null);
    // Handle success
} catch (err) {
    console.error('Command failed:', err);
    setError(err as string);
    // Show user-friendly error message
}
```

### Error Display Patterns
- Show specific error messages when helpful
- Provide actionable guidance when possible
- Use toast notifications for transient errors
- Use modal dialogs for critical errors requiring user action

## Error Recovery Strategies

### 1. Retry Logic
```rust
async fn with_retry<T, F, Fut>(operation: F, max_attempts: u32) -> Result<T, String>
where
    F: Fn() -> Fut,
    Fut: Future<Output = Result<T, String>>,
{
    let mut last_error = None;

    for attempt in 1..=max_attempts {
        match operation().await {
            Ok(result) => return Ok(result),
            Err(e) => {
                warn!("Attempt {} failed: {}", attempt, e);
                last_error = Some(e);
                if attempt < max_attempts {
                    tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(100 * attempt as u64)).await;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    Err(last_error.unwrap_or_else(|| "All retry attempts failed".to_string()))
}
```

### 2. Fallback Mechanisms
```rust
async fn operation_with_fallback() -> Result<String, String> {
    // Try primary method
    match primary_operation().await {
        Ok(result) => Ok(result),
        Err(e) => {
            warn!("Primary operation failed: {}, trying fallback", e);
            // Try fallback method
            fallback_operation().await
                .map_err(|fallback_err| {
                    error!("Both primary and fallback failed: {} / {}", e, fallback_err);
                    "Operation failed after trying all available methods".to_string()
                })
        }
    }
}
```

### 3. Circuit Breaker Pattern
```rust
struct CircuitBreaker {
    failure_count: u32,
    last_failure_time: Option<Instant>,
    threshold: u32,
    timeout: Duration,
}

impl CircuitBreaker {
    fn can_execute(&self) -> bool {
        if self.failure_count < self.threshold {
            return true;
        }

        if let Some(last_failure) = self.last_failure_time {
            Instant::now().duration_since(last_failure) > self.timeout
        } else {
            true
        }
    }
}
```

## Testing Error Scenarios

### Unit Testing
```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[tokio::test]
    async fn test_permission_denied_handling() {
        // Test with no desktop instance
        let state = create_test_state_without_desktop();
        let result = desktop_command(state).await;

        assert!(result.is_err());
        assert!(result.unwrap_err().contains("accessibility permissions"));
    }

    #[tokio::test]
    async fn test_api_timeout_handling() {
        // Test API timeout scenarios
