        assert!(result.is_err());
    }
}
```

### Integration Testing
- Test error flows in real scenarios
- Verify error messages are user-friendly
- Ensure graceful degradation works
- Test recovery mechanisms

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### ❌ Don't Do This
```rust
// Crashing the app
std::process::exit(1);

// Swallowing errors silently
let _ = risky_operation();

// Generic error messages
Err("Error".to_string())

// Panic in production code
panic!("Something went wrong");

// Unwrap without context
result.unwrap();
```

### ✅ Do This Instead
```rust
// Graceful handling
warn!("Operation failed: {}, continuing with limited functionality", e);

// Proper error propagation
risky_operation().map_err(|e| format!("Failed to perform operation: {}", e))?;

// Descriptive error messages
Err(format!("Failed to save file '{}': {}. Please check file permissions.", filename, e))

// Error handling instead of panic
match risky_operation() {
    Ok(result) => result,
    Err(e) => return Err(format!("Operation failed: {}", e)),
}

// Safe access with context
result.map_err(|e| format!("Database operation failed: {}", e))?;
```

## Monitoring and Observability

### Error Metrics
- Track error rates by component
- Monitor permission-related failures
- Track recovery success rates
- Alert on critical error patterns

### Log Aggregation
- Structured logging for error analysis
- Include request IDs for tracing
- Track error patterns over time
- Correlate errors with user actions

This error handling strategy ensures robust operation and excellent user experience across all failure scenarios.
